WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.420
You know, I don’t think there were any adjunct teachers.

2
00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:10.380
I know that I met some members of the staff, the managing editor I think was a man named C.P.

3
00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:12.620
Lighter if I’m not mistaken.

4
00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:19.020
I know I met him and the manager of the radio station, they owned WJBO at that time.

5
00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:27.280
And I . . . Actually I’m not sure whether I taught the first course in radio journalism or not but at least I had one.

6
00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:42.920
And we talked the manager of the station into giving us a half-hour once a week or maybe just fifteen minutes once a week to do a student produced show from here on WJBO.

7
00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:48.400
So we had that contact with a Manship organization.

8
00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:53.380
I understand Doug Manship was in school here at that time, but I didn’t know him until many years later.

9
00:00:53.380 --> 00:01:00.420
Did you . . . Did the students consider the radio broadcasting innovative at the time?

10
00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:03.780
To be able to broadcast a program from LSU?

11
00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:05.000
Yeah, I think so.

12
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:12.900
Because, you see, radio news really only dates back, oh, six or seven years before that.

13
00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:19.040
And there was really very little before about 1936 or ’37.

14
00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:24.000
It was still on a real state of flux at that time so that this was something quite new.

15
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:28.000
I’m not sure we did very well, but at least there was something.

16
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:32.940
What type of facilities were available to the journalism students?

17
00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:37.440
Well, at that time, it was over where the president . . . the Chancellor now sits.

18
00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:50.900
In that building, the Reveille has its presses and linotype machines and engraving facilities all in that building.

19
00:01:50.900 --> 00:01:59.960
I think that was operated largely professionally with some students working on an hourly basis as helpers.

20
00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:02.280
Facilities, there really wasn’t very much.

21
00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:05.340
I know that there was a type lab there.

22
00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:11.240
At that time apparently typography was a very important course.

23
00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:15.760
And so they had some type places enough for students to play around with that.

24
00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:21.700
Since many of them were going out to the rural press, it seems to make sense to teach.

25
00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:25.320
They had an editing lab with a horseshoe table.

26
00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:32.680
But I’m not sure if there were very many typewriters around during that period, a few but that’s about it.

27
00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:48.800
For broadcasting purposes, we got a microphone and a loudspeaker and strung a wire along the ceiling so you could put a microphone in my office and listen to it down at the . . .

28
00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:51.400
So y’all broadcasted from here?

29
00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:53.420
Well, that wasn’t broadcast.

30
00:02:53.420 --> 00:03:00.780
That was just room to room but at least it was possible to simulate a broadcast.

31
00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:05.640
Put a person at the microphone in the office and everybody else listens.

32
00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:09.880
Now it’s pretty primitive, but at least that’s about the best we could do.

33
00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:15.000
So when y’all produced the show for WJBO y’all would go down to the station?

34
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:15.200
Yeah.

35
00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:21.300
And generally the way that was assigned was that it was sort of a Reveille type of operation.

36
00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:27.600
You’d assign the students to go out and cover certain things happening on the campus or futures.

37
00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:32.600
And each student then would cover his own story and read it on the air.

38
00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:33.760
I imagine it was pretty bad.

39
00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:34.460
I’d hate to have to listen to it.

40
00:03:34.460 --> 00:03:42.800
Do you think the students were prepared very well for a career in journalism at that point?

41
00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:45.360
Or was it . . .

42
00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:46.600
Well for the types . . . yeah,

43
00:03:46.600 --> 00:03:55.460
now they were . . . I think . . . You see we were really closer to a trade school than anything very thoughtful.

44
00:03:55.460 --> 00:03:59.600
Now they were getting a college education, so it really wasn’t trade school in that sense.

45
00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:08.820
But yeah, they seemed to be able to move into a weekly newspaper fairly adequately and move through it quite well.

46
00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:16.460
Some of them went on like Jewel Claitor, for example, who wound up at the AP [Associated Press] in New York,

47
00:04:16.460 --> 00:04:21.460
and it seemed . . . I don’t know what happened to her, but she seems to have done fairly well there.

48
00:04:21.460 --> 00:04:28.180
So yeah, in terms of status of journalism at the time from a purely professional point of view.

49
00:04:28.180 --> 00:04:33.380
What we were not doing too much of, I think, was talking ideas.

50
00:04:33.380 --> 00:04:42.680
I think it was pretty well restricted to . . . I won’t call it trade school, but at least to techniques, how to do it.
